symfony/mcp-bundle: serve the 2026-07-28 lifecycle

The bundle could only build a handshake-era server: `Builder::build()` onto
`StreamableHttpTransport`. Protocol revision 2026-07-28 needs the other half of
the SDK — `buildStateless()` onto `StatelessHttpTransport` — and there was no
configuration that reached it, so the whole revision was unreachable from a
Symfony application no matter what the SDK supported.

Adds `lifecycle: handshake|stateless` per server, plus the four builder calls
that only mean something under the modern revision:

```yaml
mcp:
    servers:
        modern:
            lifecycle: stateless
            protocol_versions: ['2026-07-28']
            request_state: { key: '%env(MCP_REQUEST_STATE_KEY)%', ttl: 600 }
            cache:
                ttl_ms: 30000
                methods:
                    'tools/list': { ttl_ms: 3600000, scope: public }
            subscriptions: { bus: cache, lifetime: 30 }
            tasks: { store: cache }
```

- **`request_state`** signs what a multi-round-trip answer carries through the
  client. Without it a handler returning `InputRequiredResult` cannot remember
  anything between rounds.
- **`cache`** fills the `ttlMs`/`cacheScope` hints the revision requires on
  `server/discover`, the list methods and `resources/read`.
- **`subscriptions`** wires the notification bus behind `subscriptions/listen`.
  The `cache` bus is the one that works under PHP-FPM, where the publisher and
  the process holding the stream are different workers.
- **`tasks`** enables SEP-2663 with a store, with the same
  memory-vs-cache reasoning: the worker that creates a task is not the one
  polled for it.

A stateless server builds no session store, is left out of the session-isolation
check, and is refused at compile time if it asks for STDIO — the revision removed
sessions and is defined over HTTP.

The new `StatelessMcpController` differs from `McpController` in exactly the way
the revision does: the transport takes the dispatcher rather than one request,
because there is no session to carry anything between calls.

Note for upstreaming: this depends on SDK surface that only exists on the
`2026spec-findings` branch, so it lands after that does — the bundle's
`"mcp/sdk": "^0.7"` constraint has to move with it.

